    'DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731': "Nine bodies use focused ellipses, Kepler solving and oriented orbital elements. Several J2000 M0 values are mean longitudes and the epoch starts half a day early, so epoch credit is partial. The independent 5.145-degree, 27.322-day Moon is valid. Rings lack physical shadows. Belt CPU arrays are changed without marking GPU upload, leaving the displayed particles static. Bloom and OutputPass ACES work; atmospheres are sprite glows. Three independent comet trails exist but their head sprites never receive their orbital positions, reducing integrity. Retrograde axes and negative spin rates double-flip. Planet bodies expose sphere while picking reads mesh, causing hover/click errors; sidebar target tracking still works. Pause works, reset only restores the camera, and float postprocessing has no fallback or scene MSAA. The seven-unit Sun completely encloses Mercury, Venus and Earth: their maximum solar distance plus body radius is2.367/3.863/5.067. This reduces integrity without a fatal classification.",
    'DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-TasksAssignedByOpus5': "Planetary JPL/Kepler motion, the actual Halley orbit, bidirectional ring shadows, moving belts and postprocessing work in this offline native WebGL2 sample. The Moon incorrectly uses Earth’s equatorial frame; Triton’s signed period in the actual left-handed frame produces 23.1°. The other six moons are valid, including Charon. Uniform mean-anomaly sampling with 384 points misses the high-eccentricity Halley curve near perihelion. Lunar heliocentric/Earth-distance readouts do not use actual lunar positions, and spin pole/sign conventions are inconsistent. Desktop drag release can trigger the separate click picker, which also omits the FOV projection scale. Zoom can enter large bodies. List focus and continuous follow work; Now resets only the date.",
    'KimiK3Max': "Under the reflected(x,z,+y) world, five regular parent-equatorial circular moons have valid direction; the Moon remains on Earth equator and Triton lacks its23.1° inclination. Real fixed J2000 elements, normalized Kepler, full orientation and matching planetary lines work. Newly measured720-point uniform-M Halley paths miss the body by5.73308 display units,16.38 nucleus radii (pathFit partial, no duplicate data charge). Rings lack shadows and atmosphere is rim-only. Bloom followed by OutputPass ACES and moving belts work. Data4 remains for spin inconsistent with the reflected orbital convention and Halley phase/July claims; solar texture noise is not physical spin. Halley function stays3. Following is current and safe, global zoom1.5 penetrates large bodies, and A reset restores only the camera.",
    'KimiK3(Max)V2': "The old all-zero moon result is corrected. The global mapping is proper and the parent basis is left-handed; positive-Z moon motion therefore has the correct directed sense about the rendered pole. Six regular moons have correct parent/period but randomized±3° inclinations and receive.5 each; the Moon27.6386° ecliptic orbit and Triton179.776° remain invalid. Current JPL elements/uniform-E288 main paths, moving belts and bloom followed by ACES work. Rings only receive parent shadows, atmosphere is rim-only and Halley is absent. Independent spin and belt direction errors reduce data to4: reflected-model texture rotation has physical axis−pole (Earth156.56°), and belts oppose the proper planetary direction. Body culling is disabled, so no determinant-only integrity deduction. Pausing sets dt0 for camera interpolation too, freezing a newly requested focus/zoom; focus becomes partial confirmed by runtime. Physical pause works and A Home is camera-only.",
    'KimiK3(Max)V3': "The full final app retains the existing inputs. Real fixed J2000 elements, normalized Kepler and reflected(x,z,+y) mapping are consistent between planets and lines. The only Moon combines Ry/Rz in one XYZ Euler group: its Y is constant.241892 and the circle is offset above Earth, so it is invalid despite an instantaneous5.14°r×v angle. Halley’s360 uniform-M path misses by4.317 display units near perihelion (partial fit). Rings lack shadows and atmosphere is rim-only; bloom followed by OutputPass ACES and moving belts work. Data3.5 is revalidated by spin inconsistent with the reflected orbital convention, a1986-01-09 Halley epoch and a historical-position trail instead of a solar tail, without duplicate Moon/path deductions. Following uses exact current translations. Zoom.6 penetrates Earth1; pause freezes physical motion, decorative clouds do not count against it, and A overview reset remains camera-only.",
    'GLM5.2Max': "Eight planets follow illustrative circles with approximate orbital periods and matching paths, but no Kepler timing, orbital orientation or epoch. The moving belt, Bloom and OutputPass ACES work; rings lack physical shadows and atmospheres are Fresnel rims. The Moon has no inclination/node and an arbitrary period, so it is invalid. Spin periods are inverted twice, making Mercury rotate 58.6 times faster than Earth; Venus and Uranus also double-flip axis and signed spin. Minimum distance eight is inside the Sun’s radius twelve. Focus flights work, subsequent follow only steers the target. Zero speed stops planets, while reset only restores the camera. Float postprocessing has no fallback or scene MSAA.",
    'GLM5.3(Max)V1': "Network-loaded Three.js implements JPL secular elements, Kepler motion, matching coordinates, moving belts, bloom and final OutputPass ACES. A previous ring deduction is withdrawn: world matrices are updated before reading the ring’s world normal, whose dot product with the parent pole is 1; reciprocal shadows use consistent space and lengths. Atmosphere remains a Fresnel shell. Thirteen of fifteen equatorial satellite approximations count; the Moon inherits Earth’s equator and Triton lacks its inclined plane. Runtime checks confirmed fixed paused simMs while Phobos angle continued from 4.339 to 3.982. This stays in the existing partial pause/reset item without repeating it in moon/data credit. Venus, Uranus and Pluto reverse rotation twice, while inverse moon-local rotation cancels the orbital pivot rather than providing tidal lock, reducing data credit. Frozen J2000 guide curves deviate by much less than displayed globe radii over 2026–2100, so source staleness alone does not remove path-fit credit. Overview zoom can enter the Sun; focused tracking translates camera and target exactly. Full reset is incomplete.",
